<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of the Rio Grande</h1><p class="st-meta">1840 CE – 1840 CE · capital Laredo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Federalist breakaway republic in northeastern Mexico, proclaimed at Laredo in January 1840 and defeated within the year.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Opposition to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/centralist-republic-of-mexico">Centralist Republic of Mexico</a> ran deep in the northern frontier states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a>. In January 1840 federalist leaders meeting near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laredo">Laredo</a> declared an independent republic, naming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus-de-cardenas">Jesús de Cárdenas</a> president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-canales-rosillo">Antonio Canales</a> commander of its army, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-zapata">Antonio Zapata</a> as a leading officer.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic existed mainly as a military campaign. Its leaders hoped to restore the federal Constitution of 1824, and some sought help from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas</a>, recruiting Texan volunteers. Its government moved between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laredo">Laredo</a>, Guerrero and other border towns, and it never controlled the main cities of the region or collected regular revenues.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Centralist forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-arista">Mariano Arista</a> defeated the rebels; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-zapata">Zapata</a> was captured and executed in March 1840. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-canales-rosillo">Canales</a> surrendered in November 1840 and later served the Mexican government. The flag of the republic is still displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laredo">Laredo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, as one of the city's historic flags.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-the-rio-grande.jpg" alt="Republic of the Rio Grande" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Jose Carbajal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Rio_Grande_(historical).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1840 CE – 1840 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Laredo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jesús de Cárdenas, Antonio Canales</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>República del Río Grande</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=republic-of-the-rio-grande" aria-label="Republic of the Rio Grande on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2066 1169 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of the Rio Grande lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2386 1425L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2386 1425h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2386 1425h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Laredo. <a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=republic-of-the-rio-grande">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
